**Read:** [Polymer Physics — MIT 3.063](../sources/Polymer-Physics-MIT-3-063.lingua#r_5EPM1GX0YZDKN71N771PQCWQ5P), lectures 17–19; [Material families and mechanical response — Cambridge DoITPoMS](../sources/Material-Families-and-Mechanical-Response-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_79PNN694CQ82ENKZC6STQJ5KN9), Crystallinity in Polymers and Toughening; [Applied Mechanics of Solids — Allan F. Bower](../../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W), sections 3.5, 3.9 and 3.10.

A linear viscoelastic model cannot explain every permanent change in a polymer. Shear yielding, crazing, chain orientation, lamellar slip, cavitation, bond damage and interface debonding can compete. Crazes contain a load-bearing fibrillar structure and voids; they are not merely featureless empty cracks.

Hydrostatic stress, strain rate, temperature and environment can change which mechanism is observed. Neck propagation can change the local material state even while a global load appears steady. Elastomer softening on repeated loading and permanent set require comparison with recovery times and evidence for structural damage.

**Work:** Reconstruct one published polymer stress–strain/microstructure study. Explain its pre-yield, localized and post-draw regimes and distinguish recoverable delayed strain from persistent change. Compare an unnotched test with a constrained notch and explain why the same nominal stress does not imply the same mechanism.

